1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:04,312
Your API request crosses five
layers before it returns.

2
00:00:04,552 --> 00:00:07,390
Add the wrong one, and you'll
lose weeks debugging it.

3
00:00:07,630 --> 00:00:12,681
Which layers does your API need? By the
end of this video, you'll name each layer,

4
00:00:12,681 --> 00:00:16,438
trace three requests through them, and
know when to skip one.

5
00:00:16,888 --> 00:00:18,745
Here's the plan, in
five parts.

6
00:00:18,985 --> 00:00:23,542
One, the big map. Two, who does what.
Three, three requests traced through it.

7
00:00:23,782 --> 00:00:27,281
Four, a real run. And five, when
not to add a layer.

8
00:00:29,281 --> 00:00:32,881
Picture an airport. Check
in, security, then the gate.

9
00:00:32,881 --> 00:00:34,948
One job each, before you
board.

10
00:00:35,188 --> 00:00:40,412
A request travels the same way. A CDN
may answer straight away. A gateway

11
00:00:40,412 --> 00:00:43,775
checks who you are. The app does the
real work.

12
00:00:44,225 --> 00:00:49,394
Here is the whole map, straight from the
handbook. It starts with a client: a

13
00:00:49,394 --> 00:00:53,220
browser, a mobile app, another service,
even an AI agent.

14
00:00:53,460 --> 00:00:57,680
The client reaches a CDN or
load balancer, then an API

15
00:00:57,680 --> 00:01:00,728
gateway, then your application
service.

16
00:01:00,968 --> 00:01:06,154
And behind the application sit four
things: a cache, a database, a message

17
00:01:06,154 --> 00:01:11,761
queue with workers, and an AI gateway that
reaches the language model providers.

18
00:01:12,211 --> 00:01:16,520
The chapter draws the same system a
second way, grouped into layers.

19
00:01:16,760 --> 00:01:19,588
Edge. Gateway. Application.
Data. And AI.

20
00:01:19,828 --> 00:01:23,584
Five layers, each with a
narrow job. Client, edge,

21
00:01:23,584 --> 00:01:26,514
gateway, application, then
data and AI.

22
00:01:28,514 --> 00:01:33,067
The handbook's mental model is a company
org chart, not one person doing

23
00:01:33,067 --> 00:01:34,020
everything.

24
00:01:34,260 --> 00:01:37,704
The CDN is the receptionist.
If they already have your

25
00:01:37,704 --> 00:01:40,128
answer, they hand it over
right there.

26
00:01:40,368 --> 00:01:44,006
The gateway is security and
routing. It checks your badge,

27
00:01:44,006 --> 00:01:46,452
then sends you to the right
department.

28
00:01:46,692 --> 00:01:50,536
The application service is that
department, doing the real

29
00:01:50,536 --> 00:01:53,254
work. The database is the
filing cabinet.

30
00:01:53,494 --> 00:01:58,839
The queue is the ticket system. The AI
gateway talks to outside AI vendors.

31
00:01:59,289 --> 00:02:03,088
Zoom in on the gateway: one
front door, one address for

32
00:02:03,088 --> 00:02:05,298
web, mobile and partner
clients.

33
00:02:05,538 --> 00:02:09,335
It does the shared chores once:
authenticate, rate limit, and

34
00:02:09,335 --> 00:02:11,513
route to the right private
service.

35
00:02:11,754 --> 00:02:16,929
Fail a check, and the request
is rejected right there: a 401,

36
00:02:16,929 --> 00:02:19,898
or a 429. No service does any
work.

37
00:02:20,138 --> 00:02:24,748
And it stays dumb. A gateway that also
holds business logic stops being a

38
00:02:24,748 --> 00:02:25,672
gateway.

39
00:02:26,122 --> 00:02:29,372
The cache sits beside the app.
Reads check it first.

40
00:02:29,612 --> 00:02:33,548
A hit comes back in about 1
millisecond. A miss reads the

41
00:02:33,548 --> 00:02:36,724
database, then saves the answer
for next time.

42
00:02:36,964 --> 00:02:40,948
On a write, the app updates the
database and deletes the cached

43
00:02:40,948 --> 00:02:43,035
copy, so nobody reads stale
data.

44
00:02:43,485 --> 00:02:46,571
The queue is for work that
shouldn't make the client

45
00:02:46,571 --> 00:02:48,945
wait. Like sending a
confirmation email.

46
00:02:49,185 --> 00:02:54,966
The API validates the request, drops a job
on the queue, and answers right away.

47
00:02:55,206 --> 00:02:59,847
Workers do the slow part later. If a
worker crashes, the job goes back on the

48
00:02:59,847 --> 00:03:00,749
queue.

49
00:03:00,989 --> 00:03:05,883
That buys you decoupling, durability, and
a steady pace when traffic spikes.

50
00:03:06,333 --> 00:03:10,430
The AI gateway is one front
door for every model call. Your

51
00:03:10,430 --> 00:03:13,139
apps never talk to a provider
directly.

52
00:03:13,379 --> 00:03:18,770
It handles authentication, rate limits,
caching, routing and cost tracking once,

53
00:03:18,770 --> 00:03:23,217
for everyone. And if a provider is down,
it falls back to another.

54
00:03:25,217 --> 00:03:31,441
Request one, a simple read: GET
/products/9. It hits the CDN first.

55
00:03:31,681 --> 00:03:36,242
If the CDN has the answer cached,
it returns right there. Nothing

56
00:03:36,242 --> 00:03:38,979
behind it ever hears about the
request.

57
00:03:39,219 --> 00:03:42,372
If not, the gateway
authenticates and rate limits,

58
00:03:42,372 --> 00:03:45,021
then routes it to the
application service.

59
00:03:45,261 --> 00:03:49,009
The app checks its cache. A
hit skips the database. A miss

60
00:03:49,009 --> 00:03:51,270
queries it, then stores the
result.

61
00:03:51,510 --> 00:03:55,359
Then the response retraces the same
path, back to the client.

62
00:03:55,809 --> 00:04:01,300
Request two, a write: POST /orders. This
is the handbook's end to end view of it.

63
00:04:01,540 --> 00:04:05,979
A POST isn't cached, so it passes straight
through to the application service.

64
00:04:06,219 --> 00:04:10,998
The order service validates the body,
commits to PostgreSQL in one

65
00:04:10,998 --> 00:04:15,705
transaction, updates Redis, and
publishes a message to the queue.

66
00:04:15,945 --> 00:04:21,722
A 201 comes back in roughly 20 to 150
milliseconds. The worker sends the

67
00:04:21,722 --> 00:04:25,895
email after the response, so the
client never waits.

68
00:04:26,345 --> 00:04:31,504
Request three, an AI chat message. The
handbook traces it in seven steps.

69
00:04:31,744 --> 00:04:36,884
The gateway validates the token and
checks the rate limit. The app loads

70
00:04:36,884 --> 00:04:40,524
the conversation from PostgreSQL, and
checks Redis.

71
00:04:40,764 --> 00:04:44,972
Then the app calls the AI gateway,
which routes to an available

72
00:04:44,972 --> 00:04:48,245
provider, with a fallback if the
primary is down.

73
00:04:48,485 --> 00:04:51,649
The answer streams back
token by token, through the

74
00:04:51,649 --> 00:04:53,759
app and the gateway to the
client.

75
00:04:53,999 --> 00:04:57,501
And logging and cost tracking
happen afterwards, through a

76
00:04:57,501 --> 00:04:59,675
queue, so they never slow the
reply.

77
00:05:00,125 --> 00:05:05,976
Same map, three different paths. A read
can stop at the CDN. A write goes to the

78
00:05:05,976 --> 00:05:11,095
database and queues the slow work. A chat
goes through the AI gateway.

79
00:05:13,095 --> 00:05:18,517
Now let's watch the AI layer for
real. This is the handbook's LLM

80
00:05:18,517 --> 00:05:21,604
gateway example, a small FastAPI
app.

81
00:05:21,844 --> 00:05:27,082
Its setup is a few lines: providers in
priority order, and a token bucket

82
00:05:27,082 --> 00:05:30,814
that allows five requests, refilling
one per second.

83
00:05:31,054 --> 00:05:37,075
Over the limit, the gateway answers 429.
And every response gets a cost attached.

84
00:05:37,525 --> 00:05:42,431
Five calls to the same chat endpoint. Four
were served by the first provider.

85
00:05:42,671 --> 00:05:46,110
On the fifth, it failed, and
the gateway fell back to

86
00:05:46,110 --> 00:05:49,030
provider A, the only one that
costs anything.

87
00:05:49,270 --> 00:05:53,890
The usage endpoint rolls up cost per
tenant: five requests, a fraction of a

88
00:05:53,890 --> 00:05:54,784
cent.

89
00:05:55,234 --> 00:06:00,472
Now a burst of eight requests. The
first five pass. The next three get

90
00:06:00,472 --> 00:06:03,615
a 429: rate limited, right at the
gateway.

91
00:06:05,615 --> 00:06:10,448
Now the most important rule. Not every
API needs every layer. A small

92
00:06:10,448 --> 00:06:15,700
internal tool can be one application
service with a database behind it, and

93
00:06:15,700 --> 00:06:18,782
that's the right architecture at that
scale.

94
00:06:19,022 --> 00:06:23,912
Each layer buys you something. A
cache buys latency. A gateway buys

95
00:06:23,912 --> 00:06:28,292
central auth. A queue buys
resilience. But the price is more

96
00:06:28,292 --> 00:06:31,941
moving parts, more failure modes,
more to operate.

97
00:06:32,391 --> 00:06:38,047
Mistake one. Assuming every API needs a
gateway, a queue, and a cache from day

98
00:06:38,047 --> 00:06:43,994
one. Most don't, at first. Add a layer for
a real problem, not a hypothetical one.

99
00:06:44,444 --> 00:06:50,221
Mistake two. Treating microservices as
automatically more production grade than a

100
00:06:50,221 --> 00:06:54,928
well built monolith. It depends on the
problem. Part 11 covers it.

101
00:06:55,378 --> 00:07:00,241
Mistake three. Bolting the AI layer
on as an afterthought. It needs its

102
00:07:00,241 --> 00:07:03,186
own timeouts, fallbacks, and cost
tracking.

103
00:07:03,636 --> 00:07:09,189
A database query is usually fast and
predictable. A model call can be slow,

104
00:07:09,189 --> 00:07:14,742
non-deterministic, and billed by usage.
That's why it deserves its own box.

105
00:07:14,982 --> 00:07:22,172
So Parts 14 to 17 add LLM gateways,
token rate limits, prompt caching, and

106
00:07:22,172 --> 00:07:27,613
semantic caching. Ideas a typical CRUD
API never needed.

107
00:07:29,613 --> 00:07:30,582
Let's recap.

108
00:07:30,822 --> 00:07:34,872
A production API is a layered
system: client, edge,

109
00:07:34,872 --> 00:07:37,969
gateway, application, then
data and AI.

110
00:07:38,209 --> 00:07:41,918
Each layer has one narrow job, and
requests take different paths through

111
00:07:41,918 --> 00:07:42,785
them.

112
00:07:43,025 --> 00:07:48,364
Add a layer only for a real problem, and
treat the AI layer like the database.

113
00:07:48,604 --> 00:07:52,880
And bookmark Part 18. It's the full
production version of this map.

114
00:07:53,330 --> 00:07:58,006
Quick check before you go. A POST
/orders request must also send a

115
00:07:58,006 --> 00:08:02,752
confirmation email. How do you avoid
making the client wait for it?

116
00:08:02,992 --> 00:08:07,594
Pause and answer. Got it? Put the
email on a queue as a background

117
00:08:07,594 --> 00:08:10,523
job. A worker sends it after the
response.

118
00:08:10,973 --> 00:08:16,089
Next up: the handbook's learning roadmap,
a guided path through all 20 parts.

119
00:08:16,329 --> 00:08:20,712
Or try the chapter's exercise:
the layers a GET /orders/{id}

120
00:08:20,712 --> 00:08:24,145
request passes through, in a food
delivery app.

121
00:08:24,385 --> 00:08:26,721
Subscribe, and I'll see you
in the next one.

